<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kieu Cong Tien</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/938">938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Kiều Công Tiễn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kieu Cong Tien was a Vietnamese commander who in 937 murdered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duong-dinh-nghe">Duong Dinh Nghe</a>, the governor of the Tinh Hai circuit who had expelled the Southern Han, and took power himself. Facing a campaign of revenge by Nghe's son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngo-quyen">Ngo Quyen</a>, he appealed to the Southern Han for help, but Ngo Quyen killed him in 938 and then defeated the Han fleet at the Battle of Bach Dang.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kieu-cong-tien.jpg" alt="Kieu Cong Tien" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hoàng Hữu Xứng (1831-1905) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VietNamToanCanhDuDo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kiều Công Tiễn came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phong-chau">Phong Châu</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phu-tho-province">Phú Thọ</a>, where he was a notable of the region. The characters of his name are usually read in Vietnamese as Kiều Công Tiễn, but some works, among them the Khâm định Việt sử thông giám cương mục and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tran-trong-kim">Trần Trọng Kim</a>'s Việt Nam sử lược, read them as Kiểu Công Tiện, and Chinese histories such as the New History of the Five Dynasties write his family name with a different character.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duong-dinh-nghe">Dương Đình Nghệ</a> took over the post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiedushi">Jiedushi</a> of Tĩnh Hải quân from the Southern Han in 931, Kiều Công Tiễn became one of his generals; the Đại Việt sử lược says he was also the Jiedushi's adopted son. In the third month of the lunar year 937 he had Dương Đình Nghệ assassinated and took his post.</p><p>Nine months later Ngô Quyền, another of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duong-dinh-nghe">Dương Đình Nghệ</a>'s generals and his son-in-law, led an army from Ái province, in present-day Thanh Hóa, against him. Kiều Công Tiễn turned to the Southern Han emperor Liu Yan, who hoped to use the turmoil to regain control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozhi">Giao Chỉ</a>. Liu Yan named his son Liu Yuancao Jiedushi of Tĩnh Hải quân with the title King of Giao Chỉ and led the relief force himself, but Ngô Quyền defeated and executed Kiều Công Tiễn in 938 before the Southern Han troops arrived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bach-dang-river">Bạch Đằng River</a> that followed, Liu Yuancao was killed and Liu Yan gave up the campaign, a victory that marked the beginning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s independence from Chinese rule. For the murder of his lord and for calling in Chinese help, many Vietnamese historians denounce Kiều Công Tiễn as a usurper. His family remained powerful: after the death of Ngô Quyền in 944, his grandsons Kiều Công Hãn and Kiều Thuận were among the principal warlords of the 12 Lords Rebellion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kiều Công Tiễn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duong Dinh Nghe expelled the Southern Han in 931 but was murdered by <b>Kieu Cong Tien</b> in 937.</blockquote><a href="/state/tinh-hai-circuit">Tinh Hai Circuit</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phong-chau">Phong Châu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.28,105.44&amp;year=928" aria-label="Phong Châu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Kieu Cong Tien"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3852 5669h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3852 5669h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.28,105.44&amp;year=928">What was here in 928 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
